      "knowledge_point_id": "a363f215-ed40-504a-bcae-620b7d585678",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation When Some Points Must Be Discarded",
      "problem": "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=12x-7+\\frac{3}{x}.$",
      "worked_solution": "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =12-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}=0\n$$\n\nThe equation above has no real solutions, so there are no points of inflection.",
